4"} {"question" "e-2299" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty) = t^ng(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $g(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as $t^n g(x,y)$ for some $n\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(tx,ty) & =\\frac{1}{tx}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{ty}{tx}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{tx}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & ={t}^{-1}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & ={t}^{-1}g(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty) = t^{-1} g(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)$ is $-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044049/example-2299.json" "file_sha256" "c1eb00d8f698fd3c762dc7428fafd160ebeeab3b48557659f2cefda2ec8cd984"}] "usage" {"q-64176" [] "e-2297" [] "e-2299" [] "q-64175" [] "e-2298" [] "q-64204" [] "e-2300" [] "q-64199" []} "basis" 1638 "retractions" [[17592186148065 :question/problem "Is the function $f(x, y, z) = 3x^{2} + xy - 4z^{2}$ homogeneous? If so, what is its degree?"] [17592186148066 :question/problem "Which of the following polynomials are homogeneous?\n\n1. $f(x) = 2x + 1$\n2. $g(x, y) = x^{3} + 3xy$\n3. $h(x, y) = x^{5} - x^{2}y^{3} - y^{5}$"] [17592186148073 :question/problem "What is the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x, y)$ defined as\n\n$g(x, y) = \\frac{xy - x^{2}}{3y^{2}}$?"] [17592186148074 :question/problem "Find the degree of the homogeneous function $h(x, y) = \\frac{5y}{2x^{2} + 8xy}\\cos (\\frac{x}{y})$."] [17592186147181 :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y,z)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty, tz) = t^nf(x,y, z)\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $f(tx,ty,tz)$, and then we simplify by writing it as $t^n f(x,y,z)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(tx, ty, tz) &= (tx)^{3} + 2(ty)^{3} + (tz)^{3} \\\\\n&= t^{3}x^{3} + 2t^{3}y^{3} + t^{3}z^{3} \\\\\n&= t^{3}(x^{3} + 2y^{3} + z^{3}) \\\\\n&= t^{3}f(x, y, z)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty,tz) = t^3 f(x,y,z)\n$$\n\nand so the function $f(x,y,z)$ is homogeneous of degree $3$."] [17592186147183 :question/problem "Which of the following polynomials are homogeneous?\n\n1. $f(x,y) = x^2+x+y$\n2. $g(x,y) = x^6-2x^3y^3 + y^6$\n3. $h(x,y) = 6$"] [17592186147183 :question/worked-solution "Let's go through each of the options.\n\n- The function $f(x,y)$ is not homogeneous. It is a polynomial of degree $2$, but not all the terms have the same degree. The first term has degree $2$, while the other terms have degree $1$.\n- The function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $6$. It is a polynomial of degree $6$, and all of its terms are of degree $6$.\n- The function $h(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $0$. It is a polynomial of degree $0$, and all of its terms are of degree $0$. (There is only one term, and it has degree $0$.)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only."] [17592186147180 :question/problem "What is the degree of the homogeneous function $f(x,y)$ defined as\n\n$f(x,y) = \\dfrac{xy^2 + x^2y}{x+y}\\,$?"] [17592186147180 :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe note that\n\n- the numerator of $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $3$, and\n- the denominator of $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $1$.\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $3-1 = 2$.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nA function $f(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty) = t^nf(x,y)\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $f(tx,ty)$, and then we simplify by writing it as $t^n f(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(tx, ty) &= ((tx)(ty)^{2} + (tx^{2})(ty))/(tx + ty) \\\\\n&= (t^{3}xy^{2} + t^{3}x^{2}y)/(t(x + y)) \\\\\n&= (t^{3}(xy^{2} + x^{2}y))/(t(x + y)) \\\\\n&= (t^{2}(xy^{2} + x^{2}y))/(x + y) \\\\\n&= t^{2} \\cdot \\frac{xy^{2} + x^{2}y}{x + y} \\\\\n&= t^{2}f(x, y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty) = t^2 f(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $f(x,y)$ is $2$."] [17592186147182 :question/problem "Find the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y) = \\dfrac{1}{x}\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{y}{x}\\right)$."] [17592186147182 :question/worked-solution "A function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty) = t^ng(x,y)\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $g(tx,ty)$, and then we simplify by writing it as $t^n g(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(tx, ty) &= \\frac{1}{tx}\\cos (\\frac{ty}{tx}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{tx}\\cos (\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{t} \\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\cos (\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\\n&= t^{-1} \\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\cos (\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\\n&= t^{-1}g(x, y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty) = t^{-1} g(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)$ is $-1$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186146402 17592186146403 17592186146404 17592186146405 17592186146406 17592186146408 17592186146409 17592186146410 17592186146411 17592186146412 17592186146450 17592186146451 17592186146452 17592186146453 17592186146454 17592186146456 17592186146457 17592186146458 17592186146459 17592186146460] "immutable_fields" [[17592186146401 :answer-field/correct] [17592186146401 :answer-field/id] [17592186146401 :answer-field/key] [17592186146401 :answer-field/type] [17592186146407 :answer-field/correct] [17592186146407 :answer-field/id] [17592186146407 :answer-field/key] [17592186146407 :answer-field/type] [17592186146449 :answer-field/correct] [17592186146449 :answer-field/id] [17592186146449 :answer-field/key] [17592186146449 :answer-field/type] [17592186146455 :answer-field/correct] [17592186146455 :answer-field/id] [17592186146455 :answer-field/key] [17592186146455 :answer-field/type]